index 0000000..0be5bb2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+Static page; no build step is required. Run `npm test` for the origin-validation tests.
+
+## Messaging and URL policy
+
+Set `TRUSTED_ORDERING_URL` in `origin-validation.mjs` to the deployed ordering
+service's exact HTTPS origin. The initial value, `https://order.dailygrind.example`,
+is a reserved example domain, not a live integration. The existing Order Now link
+continues to use `/order-online`.
+
+Verified ordering messages support `{ type: 'order-status', text }` and
+`{ type: 'redirect', url }`. Status text is limited to 300 characters and inserted
+with `textContent`. Redirects and the optional `?returnTo=` link accept only HTTP(S)
+URLs on the page's schemeful site, with no embedded credentials. Relative URLs
+resolve against the current page URL.
+
+The browser's `Origin` API provides exact origin and Public Suffix List backed
+site comparisons. Without it, URL parsing provides exact origin checks, and
+cross-subdomain site checks are restricted to the explicitly configured private
+`dailygrind.example` domain. Change `SITE_DOMAIN` to the private registrable domain
+you control when deploying. On other hosts the fallback conservatively accepts
+only same-origin URLs; it never guesses the last two hostname labels.
+
+HTTP(S) links are classified with `data-external`; external links receive
+`noopener noreferrer`. Non-HTTP(S) navigation is blocked on click.
+
+The coffee widget runs with `sandbox="allow-scripts"`, without
+`allow-same-origin`. Only an `init` message from its expected window can pin its
+identity. Native `Origin.from(event)` preserves the unique opaque origin, so a
+different sandbox or a new document in the same frame cannot impersonate it.
+Browsers without `Origin` pin the expected window and check `event.source` on
+every message; they cannot detect a changed opaque document within that same
+window. Pinning is never reset by incoming messages. Widget messages carry only
+public brewing tips and target the parent's explicit origin.
+
+Tests cover URL spoofing, protocol and port mismatches, opaque origins, public
+suffix pitfalls, and widget initialization/identity. The native API tests use an
+origin-identity double because Node does not provide the browser's internal
+MessageEvent origin slot; they do not verify a browser implementation of the API.
